In Data Science, cleaning the dataset (also called data cleaning or data preprocessing) means fixing or removing anything in the dataset that can cause wrong, incomplete, or misleading results.

Here’s what data cleaning consists of, in simple terms:
✅ 0. Checking Columns name is correct or not 
 # Strip spaces – remove leading/trailing spaces in column names.
 # Fix capitalization – make all names lowercase or consistent.
# Remove special characters – brackets, $, %, /, etc.
# Replace spaces – convert spaces to _ for safe access.
# Fix typos & unclear names – correct spelling and make names meaningful.
# Handle duplicates – remove or rename duplicate column names.
# Avoid problematic names – no starting with numbers or reserved keywords (class, for, etc.).




# df.columns = df.columns.str.strip()                           # 1. remove spaces
# df.columns = df.columns.str.lower()                           # 2. lowercase
# df.columns = df.columns.str.replace(' ', '_')                 # 3. space → underscore
# df.columns = df.columns.str.replace('[^a-z0-9_]', '', regex=True)  # 4. remove symbols
# df.columns = df.columns.str.replace('__+', '_', regex=True)   # 5. fix multiple underscores
# df = df.loc[:, ~df.columns.duplicated()]                      # 6. remove duplicate columns
# df.columns = ['col_'+c if c[0].isdigit() else c for c in df.columns]  # 7. fix names starting with numbers

✅ 1. Handling Missing Values

Sometimes the dataset has empty entries (NaN, blank, null).
# Cleaning includes:
# Removing rows/columns with too many missing values
# Filling missing values (mean, median, mode, forward fill, backward fill)
# Predicting missing values using models

✅ 2. Removing or Correcting Errors

Examples:
# Wrong spelling ("Male", "male", "MALE" → unifying)
# Negative age, impossible values
# Incorrect formats (dates, currency, etc.)

✅ 3. Handling Duplicates

# Removing duplicate rows
# Identifying repeated entries

✅ 4. Fixing Data Types

# Ensuring each column has the correct type:
# Convert strings to dates
# Convert numbers stored as text into integers/floats
# Convert categories to categorical datatype

✅ 5. Dealing with Outliers

# Outliers = values far away from normal range.
## Handling includes:
Removing them
# Transforming them (log transform)
# Treating them depending on context

✅ 6. Normalization or Scaling

# Making values fall in a similar range so models can learn properly.
Examples:
## Min-max scaling
## Standardization (z-score)

✅ 7. Encoding Categorical Data

# Converting non-numeric data (like “Female”, “Male”) into numeric form:
# One-hot encoding
# Label encoding
# Target encoding (supervised)

✅ 8. Handling Imbalanced Data

# If classes are uneven (e.g., Fraud=1%, Non-Fraud=99%), you need:
## Oversampling (SMOTE)
## Undersampling
## Class weights

In summary:

Data cleaning = making your data correct, complete, consistent, and ready for modeling.